        PC Paintbrush for Windows - PBREADME.DOC 


This document provides additional information about the 
following:

- Choosing PC Paintbrush Commands
- Changing the Size of the PC Paintbrush Window
- Memory Requirements for Copying/Moving PC Paintbrush 
  Images
- Loading a Windows 2.03 Application Program Over Windows PC 
  Paintbrush
- The Meaning of the Term "Planes"
- Printing PC Paintbrush Files
- Using PC Paintbrush with Other Windows Applications

Choosing PC Paintbrush Commands

The manual instructs you to choose a command from a menu by 
pointing to the menu, pressing the left mouse button and 
dragging to make your selection.  You can also click the 
left mouse button to open the menu, and then L-click on a 
command to choose it, or L-click anywhere on the screen to 
close the menu without choosing a command.

Note:  If the Paintbrush tool is selected, you should choose 
a command by pointing to the menu and dragging.  If you 
choose a command or close a menu by clicking inside the 
Paintbrush drawing area, the Paintbrush tool will leave 
marks on the drawing area in the selected paintbrush color. 
You can also choose a menu by pressing the Alt key and a 
mnemonic key, which is underlined on the menu title.  For 
example, Alt-F will choose the File menu.  However, these 
"accelerator" keys will not work if the mouse pointer is 
either in the shape of the "Zoom" box or is a very large 
eraser.


Changing the Size of the PC Paintbrush Window

You change the size of the PC Paintbrush window in the same 
way you change the size of the window for any other Windows 
2.03 application.  For a description of how to change the 
window size, see page 66 in version 2.0 of the "Microsoft 
Windows User's Guide."  (The guidelines for changing the 
Paintbrush window size on page 2-3 of the "PC Paintbrush for 
Windows User's Manual" apply to Windows 1.03, and do not 
apply to Windows 2.03.)


Memory Requirements for Copying/Moving PC Paintbrush Images 

High-resolution color images require a substantial amount of 
memory.  When you are copying or moving PC Paintbrush 
images, you may want to close any applications you are not 
using to free up memory for Paintbrush.  If you try to copy 
or move an image, and there isn't enough memory available to 
do so, Paintbrush will alert you in a message box.


Loading a Windows 2.03 Application Program Over Windows PC Paintbrush 

When the mouse pointer is either the shape of the "Zoom" box 
or is a very large eraser, the pointer will sometimes 
appear to slide under an active application window that is 
over the Paintbrush window.  Also, should the cursor be 
either of these figures as mentioned, the <Alt><Tab> 
combination will not cycle the Windows as would be expected.  
These symptoms are a consequence of how these two special 
cursors are generated.  If they occur and are a problem, you 
can either:

      1) Reduce the size of the cursor.
      2) Make sure that one edge of the application window 
         extends beyond the Paintbrush drawing area.
      3) Pick one of the other tools.

Following either of these steps, you can then move the 
cursor into the application window.


The Meaning of the Term "Planes" 

When you load Windows PC Paintbrush, or when you clear the 
screen, a dialog box appears that tells you the size of the 
drawing area and the number of "planes" being used.  The 
number of planes corresponds to the number of colors your 
monitor can display.  One plane refers to two color 
monochrome, two planes supports four colors, three planes 
has eight colors, and four planes supports sixteen colors.


Printing PC Paintbrush Files

If you print a Paintbrush picture file on a laserjet or 
postscript printer, you may need to experiment with 
different densities (e.g., 75, 150, or 300 dots per inch) to 
get the best results.  Picture files created by color 
graphics programs can occupy a substantial amount of 
computer memory; laserjet and postscript printers have small 
memory buffers, and may not be able to handle printing an 
image at a particular size or density.

At this time, Paintbrush does not print to the Xerox 4020 
color printer.


Using PC Paintbrush with Other Windows Applications

PC Paintbrush for Windows can be used with other graphics 
applications that run under Windows. 

Paintbrush images can be copied into an Aldus PageMaker 
publication, but they have to be black and white images. To 
create a monochrome picture in Paintbrush, follow these 
steps:

      1) Select "Clear" in the Paintbrush File menu.
      2) When the dialog box appears change the number of 
         planes to one (1). 
      3) Click the left mouse button on "OK".

You can now draw pictures that can be copied into an Aldus 
PageMaker file.

Graphs created in Microsoft Excel can be edited in Windows 
Paintbrush, but cannot be pasted directly from Excel. To 
copy an Excel graph into Paintbrush follow these steps:

     1) Copy the Excel graph to the Clipboard.
     2) Paste the graph from the Clipboard into Windows 
        Paint.
     3) Copy the graph from Paint back to the Clipboard.
     4) Paste the graph from the Clipboard into Paintbrush.

You can now edit the Excel graph as you would any other 
Paintbrush file.
